WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.965
Equilibrium propagation is a promising energy-based
alternative to backpropagation for

00:00:06.045 --> 00:00:09.833
training neural networks on brains or neuromorphic
hardware,

00:00:09.913 --> 00:00:15.636
but it classically demands two things physical
substrates cannot easily deliver: perfectly

00:00:15.716 --> 00:00:18.924
symmetric weights and infinitesimally small nudges.

00:00:19.004 --> 00:00:24.533
This ICLR 2024 paper from the Friedrich Miescher
Institute and the University of Basel,

00:00:24.613 --> 00:00:27.048
by Axel Laborieux and Friedemann Zenke,

00:00:27.128 --> 00:00:32.850
cleanly separates the two resulting sources of
gradient bias and shows how to remove them.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:36.912
Finite-nudge bias is eliminated exactly with a
Cauchy integral,

00:00:36.992 --> 00:00:42.521
while weight-asymmetry bias is tamed by a new
homeostatic loss that penalizes asymmetry

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:45.100
of the Jacobian rather than the weights.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.099
The payoff: for the first time,

00:00:47.179 --> 00:00:52.837
equilibrium propagation trains asymmetric networks
on ImageNet 32 by 32 with only a small

00:00:52.917 --> 00:00:54.900
gap to the ideal symmetric case.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.602
Equilibrium propagation, or EP,

00:00:59.682 --> 00:01:04.004
is an appealing way to train neural networks
directly on physical substrates like brains or analog chips,

00:01:04.084 --> 00:01:08.407
because it computes gradients using only the
network's own relaxation dynamics.

00:01:08.487 --> 00:01:12.809
But it comes with two strict requirements: the
weights must be perfectly symmetric,

00:01:12.889 --> 00:01:17.211
and the nudge that pushes the network toward its
target must be infinitesimally small.

00:01:17.291 --> 00:01:21.613
Both are very hard to satisfy in real physical hardware.

00:01:21.693 --> 00:01:26.016
And crucially,

00:01:26.096 --> 00:01:30.418
whether weight asymmetry actually harms learning
had never been pinned down,

00:01:30.498 --> 00:01:34.820
because in practice its effect gets tangled up with
the error introduced by using a finite nudge.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.412
The reason this matters is energy.

00:01:37.492 --> 00:01:42.334
Physical neural systems, whether biological brains
or neuromorphic chips,

00:01:42.414 --> 00:01:47.660
could train networks at a tiny fraction of the
energy cost of digital hardware.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.166
But backpropagation, the workhorse of deep learning,

00:01:51.246 --> 00:01:56.896
needs a separate linear backward pass and the exact
transpose of every weight matrix,

00:01:56.976 --> 00:02:00.604
neither of which physical substrates provide naturally.

00:02:00.684 --> 00:02:05.593
EP sidesteps this, yet its own symmetry assumption
is nearly as demanding.

00:02:05.673 --> 00:02:11.391
And there was a warning sign: the asymmetric
version of EP had only ever worked on toy

00:02:11.471 --> 00:02:14.695
tasks like MNIST and outright failed on CIFAR-10.

00:02:14.775 --> 00:02:16.380
Nobody had explained why.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:20.514
This paper makes four contributions.

00:02:20.594 --> 00:02:24.347
First,

00:02:24.427 --> 00:02:28.181
it analytically separates the two sources of bias
in generalized EP: the finite nudge

00:02:28.261 --> 00:02:32.015
and the asymmetry of the network's Jacobian.

00:02:32.095 --> 00:02:35.848
Second, it extends holomorphic EP to asymmetric,
complex-differentiable systems,

00:02:35.928 --> 00:02:39.682
so the exact error can be recovered even without
weight symmetry.

00:02:39.762 --> 00:02:43.515
Third, and most practically,

00:02:43.595 --> 00:02:47.349
it introduces a new homeostatic loss that reduces
the asymmetry of the Jacobian directly,

00:02:47.429 --> 00:02:51.183
rather than forcing the weights themselves to be
symmetric.

00:02:51.263 --> 00:02:55.016
And fourth, it demonstrates that with this loss,

00:02:55.096 --> 00:02:58.850
EP finally scales all the way up to ImageNet at
thirty-two by thirty-two resolution.

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:00.725
The method has two parts.

00:03:00.805 --> 00:03:02.614
To kill the finite-nudge bias,

00:03:02.694 --> 00:03:08.157
the authors build on holomorphic EP: they drive the
network with an oscillating teaching

00:03:08.237 --> 00:03:13.763
signal and use a Cauchy integral from complex
analysis to recover the exact error vector,

00:03:13.843 --> 00:03:18.235
no matter how large the nudge and even when the
Jacobian is asymmetric.

00:03:18.315 --> 00:03:23.148
Better still, this can be estimated continuously
over many oscillation cycles,

00:03:23.228 --> 00:03:26.549
removing the need for separate free and nudged phases.

00:03:26.629 --> 00:03:28.186
To tackle the second bias,

00:03:28.266 --> 00:03:32.217
they show it grows with the skew-symmetric part of
the Jacobian,

00:03:32.297 --> 00:03:36.375
and introduce a homeostatic loss that penalizes
exactly that part,

00:03:36.455 --> 00:03:39.776
estimated efficiently with the Hutchinson trace trick.

00:03:39.856 --> 00:03:45.193
The key insight is that this improves functional
symmetry of the Jacobian without ever

00:03:45.273 --> 00:03:47.460
forcing the weights to be symmetric.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:52.026
The experiments span four datasets of increasing
difficulty.

00:03:52.106 --> 00:03:56.292
On Fashion MNIST,

00:03:56.373 --> 00:04:00.559
small multilayer networks are used to cleanly
isolate and measure each source of bias.

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:04.825
Then a recurrent convolutional architecture with
genuinely asymmetric feedback weights is trained on CIFAR-10,

00:04:04.905 --> 00:04:09.091
CIFAR-100, and finally ImageNet at thirty-two by
thirty-two,

00:04:09.171 --> 00:04:13.358
which is where the homeostatic loss really earns its keep.

00:04:13.438 --> 00:04:17.624
The authors also confirm in the appendix that the
same loss helps predictive coding networks,

00:04:17.704 --> 00:04:21.890
which have no reciprocal connections at all.

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:25.736
The headline result is striking.

00:04:25.816 --> 00:04:29.282
On CIFAR-10,

00:04:29.362 --> 00:04:32.828
the asymmetric network trained with EP but without
the homeostatic loss reaches only sixty

00:04:32.908 --> 00:04:36.375
point four percent accuracy.

00:04:36.455 --> 00:04:39.921
Add the homeostatic loss, and accuracy jumps to
eighty-four point three percent,

00:04:40.001 --> 00:04:43.467
just four point three points shy of the fully
symmetric architecture,

00:04:43.547 --> 00:04:47.013
and all with only approximate weight symmetry.

00:04:47.093 --> 00:04:50.559
Even more importantly,

00:04:50.639 --> 00:04:54.105
this is the first time this family of methods
trains at all on ImageNet thirty-two by thirty-two,

00:04:54.185 --> 00:04:57.652
reaching thirty-one point four percent Top-1 and
fifty-five point two percent Top-5.

00:04:57.732 --> 00:05:01.198
Throughout training,

00:05:01.278 --> 00:05:04.744
the homeostatic loss steadily raises the Jacobian's
symmetry and tightens the alignment

00:05:04.824 --> 00:05:08.290
between EP's error signals and true backprop.

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:12.558
The ablations cleanly separate the two biases.

00:05:12.638 --> 00:05:16.527
On Fashion MNIST, when the nudge is made large,
classic one-sided EP falls apart,

00:05:16.607 --> 00:05:20.495
its error ballooning to thirty-eight point four percent.

00:05:20.575 --> 00:05:24.463
The holomorphic Cauchy-integral estimate with six
points instead stays at fourteen point three percent,

00:05:24.543 --> 00:05:28.432
essentially matching the true derivative at
fourteen point seven percent,

00:05:28.512 --> 00:05:32.400
proof that finite-nudge bias is fully removed.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.368
Separately,

00:05:36.448 --> 00:05:40.337
dropping from the exact derivative to a coarse
two-point estimate costs about three points on CIFAR-10,

00:05:40.417 --> 00:05:44.305
quantifying the residual nudge bias.

00:05:44.385 --> 00:05:48.273
And an architecture whose output feeds straight
back to the first layer,

00:05:48.353 --> 00:05:52.242
with no reciprocal connections, benefits just as much,

00:05:52.322 --> 00:05:56.210
confirming the loss targets functional symmetry
rather than weight symmetry.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.441
To put the numbers in one place: on CIFAR-10,

00:05:59.521 --> 00:06:06.411
the homeostatic loss lifts asymmetric EP from sixty
point four to eighty-four point three percent accuracy,

00:06:06.491 --> 00:06:11.167
closing the gap to the symmetric network to just
four point three points.

00:06:11.247 --> 00:06:13.577
On ImageNet thirty-two by thirty-two,

00:06:13.657 --> 00:06:19.179
EP reaches thirty-one point four percent Top-1 and
fifty-five point two percent Top-5,

00:06:19.259 --> 00:06:21.068
the first result of its kind.

00:06:21.148 --> 00:06:23.804
And on Fashion MNIST, using a large nudge,

00:06:23.884 --> 00:06:31.360
the exact holomorphic estimate cuts error from
thirty-eight point four percent down to fourteen point three percent.

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:37.623
The takeaway is that equilibrium propagation does
not actually need perfectly symmetric weights.

00:06:37.703 --> 00:06:41.412
By removing finite-nudge bias exactly with a Cauchy
integral,

00:06:41.492 --> 00:06:47.748
and by encouraging functional symmetry of the
Jacobian with a homeostatic loss instead of the weights,

00:06:47.828 --> 00:06:54.084
asymmetric networks can finally scale to
ImageNet-level tasks with only a small gap to the ideal case.

00:06:54.164 --> 00:06:56.569
Because functional symmetry is a weaker,

00:06:56.649 --> 00:06:59.488
more achievable condition than weight symmetry,

00:06:59.568 --> 00:07:04.768
this opens a biologically plausible path to
training real physical neural substrates,

00:07:04.848 --> 00:07:08.930
and hints that brains might rely on similar
homeostatic mechanisms.
